# 8. Enforce Four Separate User Roles

Implement proper authentication and authorization for:

1. Customer.
2. Store Owner.
3. Delivery Partner.
4. Admin.

Inspect the existing authentication provider and reuse it where appropriate.

Every role must have a proper sign-in and sign-out flow, protected routes, persistent sessions, and server-side authorization.

## Customer
- Can register and sign in.
- Can browse stores and place orders.
- Can manage saved delivery addresses.
- Can view only their own orders, payments, and notifications.
- Can sign out and have their session invalidated appropriately.

## Store Owner
- Can sign in to the authorized store dashboard.
- Can view incoming orders only for stores they manage.
- Can accept or reject orders.
- Can manage authorized products, stock, opening hours, and fulfillment status.
- Cannot view or modify another store's private orders or inventory.

## Delivery Partner
- Can sign in to the delivery dashboard.
- Can view eligible offers and their assigned deliveries.
- Can accept or reject offers.
- Can update only deliveries they are authorized to handle.
- Can access the minimum customer location and delivery instructions necessary for fulfillment.

## Admin
- Has access to administrative functions only when authorized.
- Can manage users, stores, delivery partners, service areas, and assignment failures according to platform permissions.
- Public users must not be able to select Admin during normal registration and thereby gain admin access.

## Sign-in and sign-out correctness

- Prevent users from accessing protected dashboards after signing out.
- Redirect authenticated users to the correct role-specific landing page.
- Verify role permissions on the backend, not only in React.
- Do not rely on a user-editable role field to grant privileges.
- Prevent expired or revoked sessions from continuing to access protected operations.
- When a role is unauthorized, return an appropriate forbidden or authentication response.

Reuse the project's established authentication system. If the current application uses Supabase, use Supabase Auth and its authorization capabilities. If it uses Spring Security and JWT, preserve and extend that arrangement rather than replacing it.

